# for loop in awk
awk '{for(i=1;i<=NF;i++) print i,$i}' file.txt
Loop through all fields in each line — print field number and value

awk '{a[$1]+=$3} END{for(k in a) print k, a[k]}' file
Sum values by key using array, then loop to print all results

awk 'BEGIN{for(i=1;i<=5;i++) for(j=1;j<=5;j++) printf "%d*%d=%d\t",i,j,i*j; print ""}'
Pure BEGIN block — generate multiplication table without any input file

# while loop in awk
awk '{i=1; while(i<=NF){print i,$i; i++}}' file.txt
While loop — print each field with its position number

# Arrays in awk — very powerful!
awk '{count[$1]++} END{for(k in count) print k, count[k]}' file
Count occurrences of each unique value in column 1 — like sort | uniq -c

awk -F, '{sales[$1]+=$2} END{for(r in sales) print r, sales[r]}' sales.csv
Sum sales by region from CSV — group by and sum like SQL

awk '!seen[$0]++' file.txt
Remove duplicate lines — classic awk one-liner. seen[] tracks lines already printed

awk '{a[$1]=$2} END{delete a["key"]; for(k in a) print k,a[k]}' file
Delete specific key from array using delete keyword

Arrays are associative (like dictionaries)
awk arrays use strings as keys — not just numbers!
a["dinesh"] = 50000 → works perfectly

a[1] = "hello" → also works
This makes awk ideal for grouping and counting data.
